WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.239
Hi, I'm Gable!

00:00:02.239 --> 00:00:06.034
Carbon-X is a chat model you can paste raw DNA into, and

00:00:06.034 --> 00:00:07.615
it answers in plain English.

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:10.793
No separate DNA encoder.

00:00:10.793 --> 00:00:15.370
The language model just gets new words, six bases each, and one

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.896
decoder learns both alphabets.

00:00:18.842 --> 00:00:21.680
A message arrives with a question and some DNA.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.834
The tokenizer tags any run of six or more bases.

00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:27.795
English goes through the usual Qwen tokenizer.

00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:30.337
The DNA is cut into six-letter words.

00:00:30.757 --> 00:00:33.227
Both look up rows in one embedding table.

00:00:33.227 --> 00:00:37.242
The DNA rows are new, and were trained first with the model frozen.

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:41.997
Then it is the ordinary Qwen decoder, and the answer comes back in English.

00:00:43.692 --> 00:00:47.517
Because the cuts are fixed every six bases, swapping one base

00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:48.908
changes exactly one token.

00:00:49.328 --> 00:00:51.663
Insert one base and the frame shifts.

00:00:51.663 --> 00:00:53.998
Every later word becomes a different token.

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:57.032
Getting there took four stages.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.818
First, only the new DNA embeddings learn.

00:01:00.531 --> 00:01:04.510
Then three hundred billion tokens of genomes, biology and English, at a

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.831
learning rate low enough to keep English.

00:01:07.251 --> 00:01:11.261
Fine-tuning uses answers checked by code, not copied from a bigger model.

00:01:11.681 --> 00:01:15.142
Last, reinforcement learning with rewards that code can verify.

00:01:16.717 --> 00:01:21.035
On five DNA benchmarks it averages sixty-six point seven, within noise of

00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:24.994
the best closed model, and ahead of every open model tested.

00:01:26.142 --> 00:01:27.606
So the trick is small.

00:01:27.606 --> 00:01:31.119
New words for DNA, then a long, gentle run that keeps English.

00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:37.384
Six-base words, one decoder, three hundred billion tokens.

00:01:37.384 --> 00:01:39.302
And watch the reading frame.

00:01:39.722 --> 00:01:41.849
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:42.457
I'm Gable.

00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:42.761
Bye!
